family: M10
subcommand: commit
cell: commit/fsync-fault-order
setup: repo tree
outcome: refused-both
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::commit_fsync_policy_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#commit
note: the rest of the fault order and the one divergence inside it, each of them
 its own invocation. The cited test holds the whole accepted value set against
 the tool, states that the refusal stands ahead of the tree and ahead of the
 timestamp as well, states that the valueless `--fsync` takes the next word as
 its value and refuses that word, and states the divergence: with a refusable id
 and a refusable policy on one command line the tool reports the leftmost and
 the port reports the id whatever its position. The test runs both orders, which
 is what "whatever its position" takes (cli-surface.md, "P2").

family: M10
subcommand: commit
cell: commit/fsync-syscalls
setup: repo tree
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::commit_fsync_policy_controls_the_syscalls
spec: format-reference.md#the-fsync-vocabulary
note: what the policy does, which no artifact this harness reads can show: the
 policy changes the `fsync`, `fdatasync`, and `syncfs` calls alone, and leaves
 standard output, standard error, the exit status, and the repository bytes
 alike. The cited test counts those calls for both binaries with `strace` over
 the four rows of the configured-value and option-value table
 (format-reference.md, "The fsync vocabulary"): the option narrows the
 configured value and never widens it, so a repository holding `[core]
 fsync=false` syncs nothing under `--fsync=true`, and the resolved policy
 reaches the ref write and not the object writes alone. The syncing row is held
 to the target of every call -- each fanout under `objects/`, `objects/`
 itself, the repository for the `syncfs`, the ref's temp file for the
 `fdatasync`, and, for the port, the directory holding the ref -- and to the
 total the table records, 11 for the tool and 12 for the port. The test asserts
 on a host carrying `strace` and returns without an assertion on one that does
 not, which is a gate beyond the tool's own presence.

family: M10
subcommand: commit
cell: commit/table-output-counters
setup: repo tree
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::commit_table_output_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#commit
note: the counters over a growing tree, which one `run:` line cannot state: the
 cited test commits corpus C0, then the corpus plus one file, then the corpus
 again, into `archive`, `bare`, and `bare-user`, and compares the block byte for
 byte at every step, and requires the three blocks to differ from each other.
 That sequence moves `Metadata Written`, `Content Total`, `Content Written`, and
 `Content Bytes Written` apart, so the comparison reads the counts and not the
 block's shape.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# checkout -- the unprivileged mode and the subpath, Phase 17c
# (`../port-plan.md`). No oracle reads a cell's own checkout destination: the
# `manifest` oracle checks the setup's revision out itself, faithfully, into
# work space of its own. The cells that state a destination tree therefore cite
# the test that walks both implementations' destinations, and the cells here run
# the refusals, which an exit status and a message state.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: checkout
cell: checkout/user-mode
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::checkout_user_mode_and_subpath_match_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#checkout
note: `-U` applies no ownership and no extended attributes, and a regular file it
 writes keeps the sticky bit while it drops setuid and setgid; a directory keeps
 all three. The cited test states the destination trees of both implementations
 file for file, in the three modes whose checkout path differs.

family: M10
subcommand: checkout
cell: checkout/subpath
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::checkout_user_mode_and_subpath_match_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#checkout
note: a `--subpath` directory's own metadata becomes the destination root's, and
 a `--subpath` file or symlink is placed inside a destination directory the
 checkout creates at mode 0700. The leading slash is optional, and `/` names the
 whole tree. The cited test states each form against the tool's own checkout.

