 repository mode paired with a checkout form: `archive` with `-U`, `bare-user`
 with `-U`, `bare` with `-U`, and `bare` with `-H`. `-U` is the one form every
 mode accepts; the tool takes `-H` in `bare` alone
 (`cli-surface.md#p2----options-missing-from-commands-that-exist`). Each arm
 compares thirteen recommit invocations by commit checksum: the plain walk,
 `--no-xattrs`, the option alone, and the option beside `--statoverride`,
 `--mode-ro-executables`, `--owner-uid`, and `--skip-list`, each of which still
 applies over the resolved object and so reaches its own commit. A hardlinked
 copy of the checkout reaches the commit the checkout itself reaches, which
 states that the cache keys on the inode. Each arm also reads
 `Content Cache Hits` out of both sides' own `--table-output` block and holds
 the two against the count that arm resolves: 0, 13, 0, and 14. The `bare` `-H`
 arm is the one that hardlinks a symlink object, which a `bare` repository
 stores as a symlink, so its count is one above the thirteen regular files.

family: M10
subcommand: commit
cell: commit/devino-canonical
setup: repo tree
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::commit_checkout_speedup_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#commit-modifier-canonical-permissions-consume-and-devino
note: `-I` takes a resolved object verbatim, so the commit modifiers are
 skipped for every entry that resolves. The claim the cited test carries is
 agreement with the tool: it compares `-I` beside `--statoverride`,
 `--mode-ro-executables`, `--owner-uid`, and `--skip-list` over four arms --
 `archive` with `-U`, `bare-user` with `-U`, `bare` with `-U`, and `bare` with
 `-H` -- by exit status, by standard output, and by the reason on standard
 error. It does not claim that `-I` reaches the option-free
 checksum, which holds only where every entry the modifier would reach resolves.
 An `archive` repository resolves nothing, so `-I` there is the plain walk,
 which the test states by the two checksums meeting. Over a `bare-user` `-U`
 checkout `-I --owner-uid=7` and `-I` alone reach different commits, the
 directories and the symlink taking uid 7, which the test reads back out of the
 committed tree. A `bare` `-H` checkout resolves the symlink as well, because
 that mode stores a symlink object as a symlink and the checkout hardlinks that
 inode.

family: M10
subcommand: commit
cell: commit/devino-bare-user-user-mode-xattr
setup: repo tree
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::commit_checkout_speedup_matches_the_tool
spec: cli-surface.md#p2----options-missing-from-commands-that-exist
note: no cell claims that the two options leave the checksum alone, because
 over a `bare-user` repository checked out with `-U` they do not. That checkout
 hardlinks the stored objects. Each object carries the repository's own
 `user.ostreemeta` xattr, and each object inode holds the permission bits with
 the setuid, setgid, and sticky bits cleared. The plain walk reads the xattr as
 a real xattr of the source file and commits it beside the cleared bits, so the
 plain commit is the outlier. Either option resolves the object and reaches the
 checksum of the source tree itself, which is the faithful result.
 `--no-xattrs` on the plain walk drops the xattr and keeps the cleared bits, so
 it reaches a third checksum. The cited test holds the four checksums against
 each other over that mode: `--link-checkout-speedup` and `-I` reach one
 commit, which equals the source tree committed with `--orphan` at the same
 timestamp; the plain commit and the `--no-xattrs` commit each differ from it
 and from one another. Both implementations reach the same checksum for every
 one of those invocations, so the divergence this record carries stands between
 the flagged and the unflagged commit.

