note: the expression dialect, which needs a `tar=` source no setup binds. The
 tool compiles a PCRE through GLib; the port compiles the `regex` crate's
 dialect. Both carry the anchors `^`, `$`, `\A`, and `\z`, `\b` and `\B`, the
 POSIX class names in the plain and the negated form, the `\xHH` and `\x{...}`
 escapes, `\p{...}`, capturing, non-capturing, and named groups, alternation,
 the greedy and lazy quantifiers, `(?i)` at any position, and `(?i:...)`; each
 of those reaches the tool's commit checksum. Measured in both directions over
 the cited test, no expression both dialects compile is answered differently.
loss: seven constructs PCRE carries and `regex` does not, so the port refuses
 where the tool compiles: the four lookarounds, the atomic group `(?>`, the
 comment group `(?#`, `\Q...\E`, the anchor `\Z`, the single-byte `\C`, and a
 backreference inside the expression. Two run the other way, the port compiling
 where the tool refuses: a repeat count above PCRE's 65535 limit, and
 `[[:bogus:]]`, which `regex` reads as a nested class and PCRE as an unknown
 POSIX name. A refusal is compared as a refusal; the wording is the port's own
 (`cli-surface.md`, "Scope of CLI compatibility").

