note: the first line is read as bytes with no encoding requirement, and a NUL
 byte ends it ahead of the newline. The first cited test states the two forms
 that sign and the second the two byte counts. A valid key carrying one invalid UTF-8 byte
 signs, and so does one followed by a NUL and further characters; a first line of
 `AAAA\xffAAAA` reports six bytes and one of `AAAA\0BBBB` reports three. A `run:`
 line cannot state it: the harness has no setup that writes a key file.

